// A recipe, written from md-docs/recipes/add-a-picture.md by scripts/docsite/recipes.py;
// edit the recipe, not this file.
page RecipeAddAPicture(path: "/recipes/add-a-picture", title: "Add a picture, sized for every screen — Recipes", description: "Show a picture from public/ or declared with image: the build writes it at every width, sets its size, and gives it a placeholder while it loads.") {
    RecipeShell("Add a picture, sized for every screen", summary: "Put a picture in `public/` and show it with `Image`, and the build writes it again at every width a screen may ask for, sets its size so nothing on the page shifts, and fills its box while it loads. Use this for a photograph, a hero picture or a decorative image.  This page declares a hero picture with `image` and shows it with a blurred placeholder, prints its real size and colour, and shows a second picture and a decorative one straight from `public/`:", outputs: ["website", "app", "pdf", "slides"], since: "", recipes: RECIPE_INDEX, sections: [Anchor(id: "how-it-works", label: "How it works"), Anchor(id: "tune-the-image-pipeline", label: "Tune the image pipeline"), Anchor(id: "troubleshooting", label: "Troubleshooting")], related: [RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Add a dark-mode switch", to: "/recipes/dark-mode-switch"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Add video and audio", to: "/recipes/video-and-audio"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Animate a list", to: "/recipes/animate-a-list")], source: "md-docs/recipes/add-a-picture.md", how: true, hasVariations: false) {
        code {
            CodeBlock(#"image hero = "hero.jpg"

page Home(path: "/", title: "Home", description: "The front page.") {
    Heading("Home").h1
    Image(hero, alt: "The team at the launch",
          sizes: "(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 1200px",
          placeholder: .blur)
    Text("It is {hero.width} by {hero.height}, and mostly {hero.color}.")
    Image(src: "/office.jpg", alt: "The office on a quiet morning")
    Image(src: "/deco.png", alt: "")
}"#, title: "wf")
        }
        Heading("A picture from public/", id: "a-picture-from-public").h3

        Markdown("Files in `public/` are served from the site's root, so `public/office.jpg` is `/office.jpg`. Name it in `Image(src:)` with that site-relative path.\n\nGive every `Image` an `alt:` that says what the picture shows. A decorative picture takes `alt: \"\"`, so a screen reader skips it.\n\nWhen `src:` is a literal path into `public/` that names a PNG, JPEG, GIF or WebP file, the build processes the file the same way it processes an [`image` declaration](#a-picture-declared-with-image):\n\n- It writes the picture again at each width the page may ask for.\n- It sets `width` and `height` from the file, so nothing on the page shifts when the picture arrives.\n- It serves the picture as a `<picture>` element. `sizes:` and `placeholder:` work here too.\n\nThe build uses an SVG file, a URL, or a path built at run time as it is written. Give those a `width:` and `height:` when you know them, as in `Image(src: \"/hero.jpg\", alt: \"The team at the launch\", width: 1200, height: 630)`. To turn processing off, set `build.media.pipeline` to `false`.\n\nThe first image on a page loads eagerly at high priority, because it is usually the largest thing the reader sees first. Every later image loads lazily. Set `loading:` on an `Image` to choose for yourself.")

        Heading("A picture declared with image", id: "a-picture-declared-with-image").h3

        Markdown("To use a picture's real size and colour in your page, declare it with `image`, as `hero` is above. The declaration hands the build the file itself. The build looks for the path in the project's folder, then in `src/`, then in `public/`.\n\nThe build reads the picture's real size and average colour, writes it again at every width in `media.widths` that is smaller than the original, and gives the page a `<picture>` with:\n\n- a `<source>` per format, each with a `srcset` of the widths, so the browser picks the file it needs for the space `sizes:` describes;\n- `width` and `height` from the file, so the box has the right shape before any of the picture arrives and **nothing on the page moves**;\n- a placeholder while the picture loads: `.blur` inlines a copy sixteen pixels wide as a data URI, `.color` fills the box with the average colour, and `.none` does neither;\n- a content hash in every file's name, so a host can cache each file forever.\n\nThe name `hero` is also a value. You can read `hero.src`, `hero.width`, `hero.height`, `hero.color`, `hero.placeholder` and `hero.srcset`.\n\nThe attributes any element takes (`id:`, `aria-*`, `data-*`, `ref:`, an `on load` handler) go on the `<img>` inside the `<picture>`. That is where its `alt:` is and where `load` fires. The width, the height and the sources stay the build's.\n\nA PDF or a slide deck embeds the real picture rather than drawing an empty box.")

        Heading("Tune the image pipeline", id: "tune-the-image-pipeline").h2

        Markdown("To change which formats and widths the build writes, set `build.media` in `webfluent.app.json`. These are the defaults:")

        CodeBlock(#"{ "build": { "media": {
    "formats": ["webp"],
    "widths": [480, 960, 1440, 1920],
    "quality": 78,
    "pipeline": true
} } }"#, title: "json")

        Table(caption: "Tune the image pipeline") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Key").header
                    Table.Cell("What it does").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`formats`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The formats each picture is written in, best first: `webp`, `png`, `jpeg`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`widths`") }
                    Table.Cell("The widths written. A width larger than the original is skipped; the build never scales a picture up")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`quality`") }
                    Table.Cell("The quality of a lossy file. WebP and JPEG are written lossily at this quality; PNG is written losslessly")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`pipeline`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`false` copies each file as it is, as `public/` files always were") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("The build writes a file only when it saves something. A file that would be no smaller than the original is not written: the original, which the `<img>` falls back to, serves at that width instead. A format that has no width left gets no `<source>`. A JPEG that was already saved small often ends up this way.\n\nThe build caches its work in `.wf-cache/media/`, so a build that changes no image does no image work.\n\nTo see what the pipeline will make of a picture before it does it, run `wf assets --plan`:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf assets --plan /hero.jpg")
            ])

        Markdown("It prints each width and format, what each file comes to, and the widths it will not write because they save nothing. `wf assets` with no option lists every file in `public/` with where it is used, and counts the files nothing uses.")

        Heading("Troubleshooting", id: "troubleshooting").h2

        Table(caption: "Troubleshooting") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("What you see").header
                    Table.Cell("Why, and what to do").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("The page jumps when a picture arrives")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The picture is an SVG, a URL or a path built at run time, which the build cannot measure. Give it `width:` and `height:`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A01` on an `Image`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Add an `alt:` that says what it shows, or `alt: \"\"` for a decorative picture") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`wf assets --plan` lists widths it will not write") }
                    Table.Cell("Those files would save nothing; the original serves at those widths")
                }
            }
        }

    }
}
